From 89f2522184b2a21be255cf19674f802d09cfdb34 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Aleksei Bukhalov <buhaloff.aleks@gmail.com>
Date: Wed, 2 Sep 2026 05:45:32 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 8/8] fix: bound load-on-start to the RAM tier's own byte
 budget

Gate G1-MLX (24 restarts/arm, full corpus-v1) surfaced this live: the
between-session restarts (small next-session prompt, ~300-800 tokens)
were 2-4x *slower* on ram_disk than plain ram (up to 28s vs ~4s), while
mid-session restarts (large accumulated-conversation prompt) were
correctly 3-4x *faster*. The wall-clock cost on the slow restarts was
flat around 15-28s regardless of the actual prompt size -- not explained
by the request itself.

Root cause: _generate()'s load-on-start unconditionally loaded and
inserted *every* compatible disk entry before the first request could be
served. By the run's later restarts the disk store held 12 entries
(individually up to ~2 GB, ~20 GB total for an 11-session corpus), so
every restart paid for deserializing and inserting the *entire* disk
store into a 4 GiB RAM tier that would immediately evict most of it right
back out -- pure waste, and it scales with total disk-store size, not
with what the incoming request needs.

on_miss (increment 3) already covers correctness lazily: anything not
preloaded loads on the request that actually needs it. Bounded the
eager restore to what fits in the RAM tier's own max_bytes budget
(most-recently-used first, skipping anything that would overflow it)
instead of removing it outright, so a store small enough to matter (the
common case) still gets a warm start with no lazy round-trip.
---
 mlx_lm/server.py | 18 ++++++++++++++++--
 1 file changed, 16 insertions(+), 2 deletions(-)

diff --git a/mlx_lm/server.py b/mlx_lm/server.py
index fff90d6..0dec15d 100644
--- a/mlx_lm/server.py
+++ b/mlx_lm/server.py
@@ -638,9 +638,22 @@ class ResponseGenerator:
         # Load the default model if it is given
         self.model_provider.load_default()
 
-        # Rebuild the RAM cache from the disk tier, once, at startup.
+        # Rebuild the RAM cache from the disk tier, once, at startup --
+        # bounded to the RAM tier's own byte budget (most-recently-used
+        # first). Loading every compatible disk entry unconditionally was
+        # measured live on M4 to stall the first post-restart request by
+        # 10-25s once the disk store grew past a few GB: on_miss already
+        # covers correctness for anything left un-preloaded (loaded lazily,
+        # on the request that actually needs it), so there is nothing to
+        # gain from preloading more than what the RAM tier can hold anyway
+        # -- the rest would just be evicted straight back out.
         if self.disk_store is not None and self.model_provider.model_key is not None:
-            for row in self.disk_store.all_compatible(self.model_provider.model_key):
+            rows = self.disk_store.all_compatible(self.model_provider.model_key)
+            rows.sort(key=lambda r: r.get("last_used", 0), reverse=True)
+            loaded_bytes = 0
+            for row in rows:
+                if loaded_bytes + row["n_bytes"] > self.prompt_cache.max_bytes:
+                    continue
                 cache = self.disk_store.load(row)
                 if cache is None:
                     continue
@@ -650,6 +663,7 @@ class ResponseGenerator:
                     cache,
                     cache_type=row["cache_type"],
                 )
+                loaded_bytes += row["n_bytes"]
 
         current_model = None
         current_sampling = None
